# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# but the template must be committed, since .env* above would hide it
!.env.local.example

# secrets beyond env files
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx

# extra build output
.turbo/
/dist

# Downloaded planetary textures. The manifest and fetch script are the artifact, not the
# bytes; run `npm run assets 1` to restore them. Tier 2 and 3 run to several GB.
/public/textures/

# Visual verification output. Regenerate with `npm run visual`.
/artifacts/

# Generated terrain pyramids. Rebuild with `npm run terrain` after downloading the source
# elevation model named in public/terrain/<body>/manifest.json.
/public/terrain/

# Xcode and Swift build output. The project file is generated from project.yml by XcodeGen,
# so build.sh regenerates it and it is not committed.
mobile/ios/*.xcodeproj
mobile/ios/*.xcworkspace
**/.build/
**/DerivedData/
*.xcuserstate
xcuserdata/
mobile/ios/App/Resources/Textures/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Monorepo paths.
#
# Everything above this line was written when the Next app lived at the repository
# root, and the leading slashes make those rules root-relative. The app moved to
# website/ and the rules silently stopped matching: 199 files of build output, 78 MB
# of licensed textures, 24 MB of terrain tiles and 93 MB of screenshots were all being
# tracked. Nothing warns about this, because an ignore rule that matches nothing is
# indistinguishable from one that has nothing to match.
#
# A leading slash is not the only thing that anchors a pattern: a slash anywhere but the
# end does too, so `public/textures/` is still root-relative and still matches nothing.
# Only `**/` matches at any depth. That caught two of the four rules below on the first
# attempt at this fix, which is the same mistake one layer down.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Next build output and its caches. Source maps here embed absolute paths from the
# machine that built them, so this also keeps a developer's home directory private.
.next/

# Downloaded planetary textures. The manifest and fetch script are the artifact, not
# the bytes: `npm run assets 1` restores them. They are third-party and licensed
# individually, so a public clone should fetch them under those licences rather than
# receive a redistributed copy.
**/public/textures/

# Generated terrain pyramids. Rebuild with `npm run terrain`.
**/public/terrain/

# Visual verification output and probe screenshots.
artifacts/

# Native texture copies, generated from website/public/textures.
**/App/Resources/Textures/
